Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Shift-Clock At Two Kilometers
- Story & Context: Recorded illegally during the 72-hour power outage at the San José deep shaft, the track captures the exact moment Mateo Silva hit a hollow ventilation duct with a wrench while holding an low A-note on his melodica, causing the subterranean cavern to ring like a cathedral bell. The sound triggered an improvised dancing circle among thirty trapped miners who used their headlamps to cast moving shadows on the damp granite walls. As the oxygen scrubbers whined down, the men sang over the metallic clave to keep panic at bay, creating a tape that was smuggled out in a lunch pail three days later. "When the ceiling groans, we let the tempo hold us down," echoes the faint, dual-tracked unison vocal above the heavy sub-bass drive.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A dark, dramatic album cover style visual showing an antique brass clock face half-buried in glittering metallic copper ore, illuminated by an intense beam of headlamp light from below, with industrial steam rising into a pitch-black cosmic space filled with distant stars.

Track 2: ¿A Qué Hora Pasa El Satélite?
- Story & Context: Written by synth operator Tomasa Rincon after sitting on a rusted crane atop the mine tailings, waiting for the Soviet-era Cosmos 1408 debris field to cross the Atacama sky at precisely 03:14 AM. The track builds around a fragile, un-quantized melodica melody that mimics the Doppler-shifted pings of an orbiting transponder, colliding against a fierce, syncopated conga tumbao. Rincon wrote the lyrics on the back of an unpaid utility bill while watching her lover walk back toward the boarding house under the frozen milky way. "You promised me the moon, but you can’t even keep the schedule," sings guest vocalist Hector Cruz in a smooth, melancholic bolero-style croon that floats above lush, reverb-soaked pads.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A high-contrast cinematic shot of a solitary figure in a vintage leather jacket standing on a mountain ridge in the Atacama Desert, holding a glowing pocket watch open toward a night sky dominated by long, luminous satellite light-trails and a crescent moon, styled like a 1970s sci-fi film poster.

Track 3: Deferred Shift At Station Zero
- Story & Context: The closing myth-track recounts the urban legend of "Station Zero," a rumored abandoned subterranean studio where the original mine tapes were spliced with satellite radio feeds in late 1984. Built around a slow, crushing 98 BPM orbital guaguancó pulse, the song features a soaring melodica duel between Vega and an uncredited street musician known only as "El Timbalero Astral." The track ends with a minute-long ambient decay of pressurized air hiss and fading pipe strikes, symbolizing the final sealing of the mine shaft. "Cross my name off the timecard, I am staying in the deep," chants a haunting choral ensemble as the bass frequency drops below sub-audible levels.
